# Queue Buddy Week 5 Update

June 1 - June 8

- [All members] discussed and finalized the main project idea for Queue Buddy, a mobile campus-status app that helps students check crowd levels, wait times, and resource availability across campus locations such as food spots, study spaces, gyms, printers, libraries, and student services.

- [Andrew] wrote the Introduction section, explaining what Queue Buddy is, why the project is interesting, and why it makes sense as a mobile app.

- [Marco] worked on the Use Cases section, including the use case diagram and explanations of the main user interactions, such as checking location status, finding a suitable location, submitting reports, flagging inaccurate reports, and removing flagged reports.

- [Amy] wrote the User Scenarios section, focusing on the three main situations the app will support: finding food quickly between classes, finding a quiet study space during peak hours, and finding an available printer before a deadline.

- [Karim] wrote the Requirements and Timeline section, including the functional requirements, non-functional requirements, implementation timeline, and Gantt diagram for the project.

- [Oscar] wrote the Design Considerations section, discussing human values, privacy, report accuracy, moderation, accessibility, and possible unintended harms.

- [Ayo] worked on the Acknowledgments and References section, including references to course materials and tools used during the proposal process.

- [All members] reviewed the full P3 proposal for consistency, formatting, and alignment with the project requirements before preparing it for submission.
